      <section class="tg-section" id="question">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Question</h2>

        <p>Few questions divide sincere Christians as sharply as this one: if someone attacks me or my
          family, may I defend myself, and with what? Some answer from Jesus' call to turn the other
          cheek. Others answer from the duty to protect the innocent. Both start from Scripture, and
          both want to honor Christ.</p>

        <p>This study does not pretend the question is simple. It looks at what the Bible says about
          defending life, reads Jesus' hard sayings in their context, and sets out the limits that
          every follower of Christ should keep: restraint, proportion, love for the enemy, and a
          readiness to flee or de-escalate first.</p>

        <p>Its conclusion is that Scripture permits the defense of life, and for the vulnerable in our
          care sometimes expects it, while honoring the brothers and sisters who conscientiously
          choose non-resistance for themselves. It is not legal advice. Self-defense law differs from
          place to place, so learn yours.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Translation</span>
          Scripture quotations on this page are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB).
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="scripture">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What Scripture Says About Defending Life</h2>

        <p>The Bible never has a chapter titled "self-defense." But it speaks to the matter in the
          law, in the histories, in the wisdom books, and in the words of Jesus. Taken together, the
          picture is consistent.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Law: A Homeowner Is Not a Murderer</h3>

        <p>"If a thief is caught breaking in and is beaten to death, no one shall be guilty of
          bloodshed. But if it happens after sunrise, there is guilt for his bloodshed"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 22:2-3">Exodus 22:2–3</a>). The law does not treat a person
          who kills an intruder as a murderer. It also draws a line: in the dark, the householder
          cannot know whether his life is at stake. By daylight he can see what he faces, and the
          force he uses must fit the threat. Defense is permitted. Excess is not.</p>

        <p>The law also takes the victim's side.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 22:25-27">Deuteronomy 22:25–27</a> describes a woman
          attacked in open country and says she "has committed no sin worthy of death," and that the
          case "is just like one in which a man attacks his neighbor and murders him." Scripture
          places the guilt on the aggressor, never on the person who cries out or resists.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Histories: Faithful People Took Up Arms to Protect Others</h3>

        <p>When Abram heard that his relative Lot had been captured, he "mobilized the 318 trained men
          born in his household" and "retrieved all the goods, as well as his relative Lot and his
          possessions, together with the women and the rest of the people"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 14:14-16">Genesis 14:14–16</a>). Nothing in the text
          suggests that he sinned.</p>

        <p>When enemies threatened Jerusalem, Nehemiah armed the builders, stationing men "by families
          with their swords, spears, and bows." Then he told the people, "Do not be afraid of them.
          Remember the Lord, who is great and awesome, and fight for your brothers, your sons and your
          daughters, your wives and your homes"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Nehemiah 4:13-14">Nehemiah 4:13–14</a>). Notice the order: remember
          the Lord first, then fight for your family. The workers "worked with one hand and held a
          weapon with the other" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Nehemiah 4:17">Nehemiah 4:17</a>). The wall
          got built, and it was defended.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Wisdom Books: Rescuing the Endangered Is a Duty</h3>

        <p>"Rescue those being led away to death, and restrain those stumbling toward the slaughter. If
          you say, 'Behold, we did not know about this,' does not He who weighs hearts consider it?"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 24:11-12">Proverbs 24:11–12</a>). The psalmist says the
          same: "Defend the cause of the weak and fatherless; uphold the rights of the afflicted and
          oppressed. Rescue the weak and needy; save them from the hand of the wicked"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 82:3-4">Psalm 82:3–4</a>). A person who stands by while an
          innocent is murdered, when he could have stopped it safely, answers to God for it.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 25:26">Proverbs 25:26</a> adds a warning of its own: "Like
          a muddied spring or a polluted well is a righteous man who gives way to the wicked."</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The State Bears the Sword, and Christians May Support It</h3>

        <p>Paul says the ruler "does not carry the sword in vain. He is God's servant, an agent of
          retribution to the wrongdoer" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:4">Romans 13:4</a>). God has
          given civil government the task of restraining violent people. That is why calling the
          police is the right first step for a Christian, and why a believer may serve as an officer or
          soldier. John the Baptist did not tell the soldiers who came to him to leave their
          profession (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 3:14">Luke 3:14</a>). But the state cannot be
          everywhere, and the minutes before help arrives are often the ones that matter.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Jesus and the Swords</h3>

        <p>On the night of His arrest, Jesus said to the disciples, "the one with a purse should take
          it, and likewise a bag; and the one without a sword should sell his cloak and buy one." When
          they answered, "Look, Lord, here are two swords," He said, "That is enough"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:36">Luke 22:36</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:38">38</a>). Christians read this differently. Some take it
          literally: Jesus assumes His followers may carry the means of defense. Others take it as a
          figure of the hostility they were about to face, noting that He then rebuked the use of a
          sword at His arrest. This text alone cannot settle the question, and it is not the main
          pillar of the case. It does show that the New Testament does not treat a sword in a
          disciple's hands as inherently sinful.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="cheek">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Jesus and Turning the Other Cheek</h2>

        <p>The strongest objection to defending yourself comes from the Sermon on the Mount, and it
          deserves a careful hearing.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What Jesus Actually Said</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          You have heard that it was said, 'Eye for eye and tooth for tooth.' But I tell you not to
          resist an evil person. If someone slaps you on your right cheek, turn to him the other also;
          if someone wants to sue you and take your tunic, let him have your cloak as well; and if
          someone forces you to go one mile, go with him two miles. Give to the one who asks you, and
          do not turn away from the one who wants to borrow from you.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:38-42">Matthew 5:38–42</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Look at the four examples Jesus gives. A slap on the right cheek is most naturally a
          backhanded insult, an injury to honor. A lawsuit over a tunic is a dispute over property. A
          soldier forcing you to carry his pack is an imposition on your time. A request for a loan is
          a request for money. None of these is an attack on a person's life. Jesus is teaching us to
          give up our rights, our dignity, and our possessions rather than to retaliate, and to answer
          hostility with generosity. That teaching is binding on every believer.</p>

        <p>Many Christians read the passage as a ban on revenge and on clinging to our rights, not as a
          ruling on what to do when a killer is at the door or a child is being attacked. Others,
          especially in the Anabaptist tradition, hear in it a call to non-resistance in every
          circumstance, and they have lived and died by that conviction. This study takes the first
          reading, but the second comes from people trying to obey Jesus, and it should be treated
          with respect.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Defense Is Not Vengeance</h3>

        <p>Paul builds on the same teaching: "Do not repay anyone evil for evil. . . . If it is
          possible on your part, live at peace with everyone. Do not avenge yourselves, beloved, but
          leave room for God's wrath. For it is written: 'Vengeance is Mine; I will repay, says the
          Lord.' . . . Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:17-21">Romans 12:17–21</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Key Distinction</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The key distinction is between <em>defending</em> and
            <em>avenging</em>. Defense stops an attack that is happening now. Vengeance repays a wrong
            that is already over. Scripture forbids the second to private persons, and a believer's
            heart must be kept clear of it.</p>
        </div>

        <p>David learned this when Abigail stopped him from slaughtering Nabal's household. He blessed
          God for keeping him "from bloodshed and from avenging myself by my own hand"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 25:33">1 Samuel 25:33</a>). His sin there was not
          defense. It was vengeance.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Peter's Sword</h3>

        <p>When the arrest party came, one of Jesus' companions "drew his sword and struck the servant
          of the high priest, cutting off his ear." "Put your sword back in its place," Jesus said to
          him. "For all who draw the sword will die by the sword"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:51-52">Matthew 26:51–52</a>). John identifies the
          swordsman as Peter and records Jesus' reason: "Shall I not drink the cup the Father has
          given Me?" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 18:10-11">John 18:10–11</a>). Jesus also told
          Pilate, "My kingdom is not of this world; if it were, My servants would fight to prevent My
          arrest" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 18:36">John 18:36</a>).</p>

        <p>This scene is often quoted against all defense, but notice what was happening. Peter was not
          protecting an innocent from murder. He was trying to stop the very arrest that God had
          ordained to save the world, and he was fighting to advance a kingdom that does not advance
          by the sword. Jesus' warning is against making violence our way of life. And look at how
          Jesus handled the wounded man: "He touched the man's ear and healed him"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:51">Luke 22:51</a>). Even in the hands of His enemies, He
          showed mercy to the one who came to take Him. That is the spirit in which a Christian should
          face any attacker.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="disagreement">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Yourself, Others, and Honest Disagreement</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What I May Give Up, and What I May Not</h3>

        <p>Jesus' hard sayings in Matthew 5 are about what I give up of my own: my cheek, my cloak, my
          mile. The wisdom books speak of what I owe to others: "Rescue those being led away to death"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 24:11">Proverbs 24:11</a>). Those are different duties. I
          may waive my own claims, but I have no right to waive someone else's life.</p>

        <p>This is why many Christians who hesitate to defend themselves still believe that they must
          defend a child, a spouse, a congregation, or a stranger being beaten in the street.
          Nehemiah's call was to "fight for your brothers, your sons and your daughters, your wives
          and your homes" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Nehemiah 4:14">Nehemiah 4:14</a>). A father or
          mother who stood by while an intruder killed their children, and called it turning the other
          cheek, would have mistaken cowardice for holiness. Defending the people entrusted to us is
          love in action.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Choice to Suffer Is Also Biblical</h3>

        <p>There is a second strand in Scripture, and it is not weakness. Peter tells persecuted
          believers, "Do not repay evil with evil or insult with insult, but with blessing, because to
          this you were called" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 3:9">1 Peter 3:9</a>). He holds up
          Christ as the pattern: "When they heaped abuse on Him, He did not retaliate; when He
          suffered, He made no threats, but entrusted Himself to Him who judges justly"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:23">1 Peter 2:23</a>). Stephen, as the stones struck him,
          prayed, "Lord, do not hold this sin against them"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 7:60">Acts 7:60</a>).</p>

        <p>When a Christian is attacked <em>for being a Christian</em>, the New Testament's dominant
          note is patient, forgiving endurance, and many martyrs have followed it. A believer who in
          conscience chooses never to use force, even to save his own life, is not disobeying God. He
          may be obeying Him more strictly than the rest of us.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Christians Have Never Agreed</h3>

        <p>The church has not spoken with one voice here. The just-war tradition running from
          Augustine through Aquinas, and the major Reformers after them, taught that using force to
          protect the innocent can be an act of love. The Anabaptist, Mennonite, and Quaker traditions
          have taught non-resistance as the way of Jesus, and have paid for it. Faithful believers
          stand on both sides, and each side can point to texts.</p>

        <p>Paul's counsel on disputed matters offers a model, though he was speaking about food and
          holy days, so we apply it by analogy: "Each one should be fully convinced in his own mind"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:5">Romans 14:5</a>). And: "Keep your belief about such
          matters between yourself and God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:22">Romans 14:22</a>).
          Where Scripture leaves room, we should hold our conviction firmly for ourselves and
          graciously toward those who differ. Nobody should use this question to belittle a brother or
          sister, whether the one who carries a weapon or the one who will not.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="limits">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Heart and the Limits</h2>

        <p>Where defense is permitted, <em>how</em> it is done still matters to God. A Christian who
          defends his family should look different from a man who simply enjoys a fight. These
          principles keep defense from turning into something else.</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Prepare the heart before the moment.</strong> "A patient man has great
            understanding, but a quick-tempered man promotes folly"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 14:29">Proverbs 14:29</a>). James traces violence to
            "the passions at war within you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 4:1">James 4:1</a>). "Be
            angry, yet do not sin" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:26">Ephesians 4:26</a>). Ask
            now whether you are quick to take offense, because the moment of danger is the wrong time
            to find out. Disciples are told, "Turn away from evil and do good; seek peace and pursue
            it" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 34:14">Psalm 34:14</a>), and to "Pursue peace with
            everyone, as well as holiness" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:14">Hebrews 12:14</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid, de-escalate, and flee first.</strong> "A gentle answer turns away wrath,
            but a harsh word stirs up anger" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 15:1">Proverbs 15:1</a>).
            "The prudent see danger and take cover"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 22:3">Proverbs 22:3</a>). Jesus told persecuted
            disciples, "When they persecute you in one town, flee to the next"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 10:23">Matthew 10:23</a>). Paul escaped Damascus when
            "his disciples took him and lowered him in a basket through a window in the wall"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 9:25">Acts 9:25</a>). "If it is possible on your part, live
            at peace with everyone" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:18">Romans 12:18</a>). Most
            conflicts that Christians call self-defense are really pride, road rage, or a quarrel we
            could have walked away from. Walking away from those is the first form of obedience.</li>
          <li><strong>Use only the force the threat requires.</strong>
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 22:2-3">Exodus 22:2–3</a> draws a line between the
            night-time intruder and the thief seen in daylight. Force is for stopping an attack, not
            for punishing the attacker. When the threat has ended, the justification has ended with
            it.</li>
          <li><strong>Use lawful protection when it is available.</strong> When Paul's life was
            threatened by a conspiracy, his nephew reported it, and Paul sent him to the Roman
            commander, who arranged soldiers "to take him safely to Governor Felix"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 23:16-24">Acts 23:16–24</a>). Calling the police, locking
            doors, and leaving a dangerous situation are all more faithful than improvising.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep loving the one who attacks you.</strong> "Love your enemies and pray for
            those who persecute you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:44">Matthew 5:44</a>). "Do not
            gloat when your enemy falls" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 24:17">Proverbs 24:17</a>).
            "Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:21">Romans 12:21</a>). A believer who has had to stop
            an attacker should afterward call for help for him, render aid if it is safe, and pray for
            him. Jesus touched the ear of the man who came to arrest Him and healed it.</li>
          <li><strong>Stay unafraid.</strong> Peter writes, "Do not fear what they fear; do not be
            shaken" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 3:14">1 Peter 3:14</a>). Christians are not meant
            to live in suspicion or panic. Readiness that grows out of fear is a poor foundation.
            Readiness that grows out of love for the people God has given us, and trust in Him, is a
            good one.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="hard-questions">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Hard Questions</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Is Lethal Force Ever Justified?</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 22:2">Exodus 22:2</a> says that when a thief is caught
          breaking in and is beaten to death, "no one shall be guilty of bloodshed." God's law
          therefore recognizes that a defender may sometimes take a life without becoming a murderer.
          At the same time, Scripture treats human life as sacred, because "in His own image God has
          made mankind" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 9:6">Genesis 9:6</a>). The reason defense is
          permitted is the same reason murder is condemned: a human life is precious. Lethal force is
          the last resort, to be used only when nothing less will stop an attack on a life, and never
          casually.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What About Property?</h3>

        <p>Jesus tells us to let go of what is ours: "if someone wants to sue you and take your tunic,
          let him have your cloak as well" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:40">Matthew 5:40</a>).
          He also warns, "one's life does not consist in the abundance of his possessions"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 12:15">Luke 12:15</a>). The Christian default is to let
          property go. Use of force to protect a television, a car, or a wallet fits poorly with those
          words. Where a break-in happens at night and you cannot know whether lives are at risk, as
          in <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 22:2">Exodus 22:2</a>, the situation is different,
          because you are protecting the people in the house. Be aware that laws about property and
          force vary widely, and that what the law allows may exceed what love requires.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What About Firearms and Training?</h3>

        <p>The Bible says nothing about guns, so we reason from principles. Wisdom says, "A wise man
          fears and turns from evil, but a fool is careless and reckless"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 14:16">Proverbs 14:16</a>). A believer who chooses to keep
          a weapon for the defense of his household takes on a serious stewardship: training, secure
          storage away from children, knowledge of the law, and a settled resolve about when it would
          and would not be used. A weapon kept carelessly endangers the very people it is meant to
          protect. A weapon carried as a source of confidence, in place of trust in God, has become
          something like an idol. Many believers prayerfully decide not to keep one, and that is a
          legitimate decision too.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What If I Have to Use Force?</h3>

        <p>Even a justified act can leave a person shaken, grieving, or haunted. That is not proof of
          sin. It is the cost of having been near death. Bring the weight to God: "Cast your burden
          upon the LORD and He will sustain you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 55:22">Psalm 55:22</a>).
          Talk to your pastor and, if needed, a Christian counselor, because trauma is real and
          bearing it alone is not a mark of faith. If you struggle because you acted in anger or went
          beyond what was necessary, then confess it, for "He is faithful and just to forgive us our
          sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:9">1 John 1:9</a>). Tell the truth to the police and in
          court, and forgive the person who forced you to act.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What If I Freeze, or Choose Not to Act?</h3>

        <p>Some people cannot act in a crisis, and some decide in advance that they never will. Neither
          the believer who defended his family nor the believer who could not is outside the reach of
          God's grace. We are not saved by what we did in our worst night. We are saved by Christ, who
          "entrusted Himself to Him who judges justly"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:23">1 Peter 2:23</a>), and who now holds us.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="prayer">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Prayer</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Lord, You are my refuge and the keeper of all who are in my care. Make me peaceable, slow to
          anger, and quick to walk away from needless conflict. Teach me to love my enemies and to pray
          for those who would harm me. Give me wisdom to see danger and take cover, courage to protect
          the weak, and restraint to use no more force than love allows. If the hour ever comes, keep
          my hands steady, my heart clean, and my trust in You. Whatever I face, let me entrust myself
          to You who judge justly. In Jesus' name, amen.
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Peacemakers, Not Pushovers</h3>

        <p>"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called sons of God"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:9">Matthew 5:9</a>). The Christian approach to
          self-defense begins with peace: peace in the heart, peace with neighbors, peace pursued at
          real cost. It gives up rights, property, and pride without a fight. And it recognizes that
          love for the vulnerable sometimes means standing between a violent person and the one he
          would hurt, and doing so with sorrow, restraint, and prayer.</p>

        <p>If you hold that you may defend yourself, do so as a disciple. If you hold that you may not,
          do so as a disciple also. In both cases, the aim is the same: to look like Christ, who was
          gentle with the guilty, protective of the weak, and entrusted every outcome to the Father.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="verses">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Key Verses at a Glance</h2>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Theme</th><th>Passage</th><th>What it teaches</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Defense of the home</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 22:2-3">Exodus 22:2–3</a></td><td>No bloodguilt for a night intruder; by day, force must fit the threat</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Guilt lies with the attacker</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 22:25-27">Deuteronomy 22:25–27</a></td><td>The victim who cries out or resists is not blamed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Armed rescue</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 14:14-16">Genesis 14:14–16</a></td><td>Abram mobilizes to rescue Lot</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Protecting family</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Nehemiah 4:13-14">Nehemiah 4:13–14</a>, <a class="ref" data-ref="Nehemiah 4:17">17</a></td><td>Remember the Lord, then fight for your families</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Duty to rescue</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 24:11-12">Proverbs 24:11–12</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 82:3-4">Psalm 82:3–4</a></td><td>Rescue the endangered; God knows if you stood by</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Role of the state</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:4">Romans 13:4</a></td><td>The ruler bears the sword as God's servant</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Swords</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:36-38">Luke 22:36–38</a></td><td>Debated; does not treat a sword as inherently sinful</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Giving up my rights</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:38-42">Matthew 5:38–42</a></td><td>Answer insult and loss with generosity, not retaliation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No vengeance</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:17-21">Romans 12:17–21</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 25:32-34">1 Samuel 25:32–34</a></td><td>Defense stops an attack; vengeance repays a past one</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Peter's sword</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 26:51-52">Matthew 26:51–52</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="John 18:10-11">John 18:10–11</a>, <a class="ref" data-ref="John 18:36">36</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:51">Luke 22:51</a></td><td>The kingdom does not advance by the sword; Jesus healed the wounded man</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Suffering like Christ</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:21-23">1 Peter 2:21–23</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 3:9">3:9</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 7:59-60">Acts 7:59–60</a></td><td>Patient endurance is a faithful path</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Avoid and flee</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 15:1">Proverbs 15:1</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 22:3">22:3</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 10:23">Matthew 10:23</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 9:23-25">Acts 9:23–25</a></td><td>De-escalate and escape first</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lawful protection</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 23:16-24">Acts 23:16–24</a></td><td>Use the authorities God has provided</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Love the enemy</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 5:44">Matthew 5:44</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 24:17">Proverbs 24:17</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:21">Romans 12:21</a></td><td>Overcome evil with good</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Disputed matters</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:5">Romans 14:5</a>, <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:22">22</a></td><td>Be fully convinced; do not belittle those who differ</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Life and possessions</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 9:6">Genesis 9:6</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 12:15">Luke 12:15</a></td><td>Life is sacred; property is not worth a life</td></tr>
            <tr><td>After the event</td><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 55:22">Psalm 55:22</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:9">1 John 1:9</a></td><td>Bring the burden and any sin to God</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

